do $night_polish$
declare updated_count integer;
begin
 with updated as (
  update public.pll_activities a set instructions=e.next
  from jsonb_to_recordset($staged$[{"slug":"gauntlet-practice","prior":"### Practice choosing a project direction\n\nIn this module, identify interests or questions you care about, evaluate two project ideas, and choose a focused direction. The Gauntlet lets you practice with other learners' situations before applying the criteria to your own project.\n\n### Before you play\n\nMeaning asks why a project matters. Doability asks whether the learner can pursue it with the resources and time available. Focus asks whether its scope is clear enough to begin.\n\nWatch for one choice that sounds promising but runs into a constraint. Think about what you would change to make the next step more useful.\n\n### Play the Project Launch Gauntlet\n\nOpen the game in a new tab. Choose a direction for each case, read the explanatory feedback, then return to this lesson.\n\nhttps://project-launch-gauntlet.vercel.app/\n\n### Apply the criteria to your ideas\n\nDescribe two ideas connected to an interest of your own. Compare each for personal relevance, feasibility, and scope. Name the direction you prefer and a tradeoff you accept.\n\nAsk a peer or facilitator to identify one assumption or constraint you may have missed. Use that feedback to narrow the direction or explain why you kept it. Carry the comparison and your final direction into the Project Direction Brief.","next":"### Practice choosing a project direction\n\nIn this module, identify interests or questions you care about, evaluate two project ideas, and choose a focused direction. The Gauntlet lets you practice with other learners' situations before applying the criteria to your own project.\n\n### Before you play\n\nMeaning asks why a project matters. Doability asks whether the learner can pursue it with the resources and time available. Focus asks whether its scope is clear enough to begin.\n\nWatch for one choice that sounds promising but runs into a constraint. Think about what you would change to make the next step more useful.\n\n### Choose the game or text case\n\nIf the game works for you, open it in a new tab, try a case, read the explanatory feedback, and return here. If you prefer text or cannot use the game, go to Compare Two Directions and use Devon’s park case. Either route prepares you for the same comparison.\n\nhttps://project-launch-gauntlet.vercel.app/\n\n### Carry one idea forward\n\nNotice one constraint or tradeoff that changed the decision. You can jot it down here or keep it in your own notes. Complete your personal comparison in Compare Two Directions.","is_published":true},{"slug":"first-move-studio","prior":"Work through Ari’s three decisions. For each one, choose a response and read the feedback. Then use the same reasoning on your own project.\n\n[[first-move-studio]]\n\n### Apply it to your project\n\nUse the direction you chose in Module 1. Draft a response with your project direction, the first question you need to answer, one safe action you can take with the resources you have, and what you expect to observe. State one thing that observation could tell you and one thing that would remain uncertain.\n\nShare your proposed test in the course discussion or use the facilitator feedback route. When reviewing a peer, ask whether their action can answer their question, whether it fits their constraints, and what they could observe. Give one specific suggestion. If peer feedback is unavailable, send your plan to the facilitator.\n\nRevise or defend your first action after feedback. Save your reasoning for the Launch Portfolio, where you’ll document what you tried or what blocked you and justify the next step.","next":"Work through Ari’s three decisions. For each one, choose a response and read the feedback. Then use the same reasoning on your own project.\n\n[[first-move-studio]]\n\n### Carry the reasoning into your project\n\nPick one idea from Ari’s choices that could improve your own first move. Think about one safe action and what you might observe. Use that thinking in the next activity, Create — First Move Card, where you will draft and share your personal plan for facilitator feedback.\n\nIf you want another perspective, share a brief proposed test in the Peer feedback forum. Leave out personal information. When replying, name one strength and one feasible improvement. Keep your full plan and your facilitator’s feedback in the private course response and Feedback areas.","is_published":true},{"slug":"module-one-introduction","prior":"# Welcome to Module 1: Discover & Define\n\nYou may have several interests and questions worth exploring. This module will help you narrow those possibilities without losing the reasons they matter to you. You will learn three lenses—relevance, feasibility and scope—then observe an example, try guided decisions, and compare two possibilities for your own project. After feedback, you'll submit a Project Direction Brief. The goal is a reasoned choice, not a perfect answer.\n\n## Assigned reading — Three Lenses, One Decision\n\nAn interest is a starting point, not yet a project plan. “I like art,” for example, can lead to many directions: learning a drawing technique, making illustrations to explain a topic, or designing a small display for a school club. Your task is not to prove that one of these is objectively the best idea. It is to make a choice you can explain.\n\n**Relevance:** Why does this direction matter to you or to a community you care about? Personal relevance can come from curiosity, usefulness, creativity, or a question you'd like to answer. A meaningful reason doesn't need to be dramatic.\n\n**Feasibility:** What would it take to begin? Consider time, supplies, permissions, information and support. A project can matter a great deal and still be difficult to start right now. That doesn't make the idea bad; it may suggest a smaller first step.\n\n**Scope:** How big is the idea? “Improve school lunches” is broad. “Ask five willing students which lunch menu information is hardest to find, without collecting sensitive personal details” is narrower and creates a specific question. Focus lets you gather evidence without claiming to solve the whole problem at once.\n\nA **tradeoff** is something you accept when choosing one option over another. Maybe an idea is personally exciting but requires materials you don't have. Another idea may be less ambitious but easier to test. A strong decision recognizes both strengths and limitations.\n\n**Try this:** Write two possible directions. For each, name one reason it matters, one constraint, and one way to narrow it. Which direction would you investigate first, and what could make you reconsider?\n\nUse this reading to revisit the three decision lenses. The interactive lesson and Gauntlet will give you practice, and the Project Direction Brief will show how you use the lenses for your own idea.\n\nContinue to Your Starting Point. Return to this reading whenever you need support.\n\n### By the end of this module\n\n- Identify at least two interests or questions that could become a project.\n- Apply relevance, feasibility, and scope to compare two possible directions.\n- Justify a focused direction and explain a tradeoff in your Project Direction Brief.\n\nAfter the worked lesson, try the Gauntlet or use the included text case in Compare Two Directions. Carry a specific question from your Direction Brief into Module 2.","next":"# Welcome to Module 1: Discover & Define\n\nYou may have several interests and questions worth exploring. This module will help you narrow those possibilities without losing the reasons they matter to you. You will learn three lenses—relevance, feasibility and scope—then observe an example, try guided decisions, and compare two possibilities for your own project. After feedback, you'll submit a Project Direction Brief. The goal is a reasoned choice, not a perfect answer.\n\n## Assigned reading — Three Lenses, One Decision\n\nAn interest is a starting point, not yet a project plan. “I like art,” for example, can lead to many directions: learning a drawing technique, making illustrations to explain a topic, or designing a small display for a school club. Your task is not to prove that one of these is objectively the best idea. It is to make a choice you can explain.\n\n**Relevance:** Why does this direction matter to you or to a community you care about? Personal relevance can come from curiosity, usefulness, creativity, or a question you'd like to answer. A meaningful reason doesn't need to be dramatic.\n\n**Feasibility:** What would it take to begin? Consider time, supplies, permissions, information and support. A project can matter a great deal and still be difficult to start right now. That doesn't make the idea bad; it may suggest a smaller first step.\n\n**Scope:** How big is the idea? “Improve school lunches” is broad. “Ask five willing students which lunch menu information is hardest to find, without collecting sensitive personal details” is narrower and creates a specific question. Focus lets you gather evidence without claiming to solve the whole problem at once.\n\nA **tradeoff** is something you accept when choosing one option over another. Maybe an idea is personally exciting but requires materials you don't have. Another idea may be less ambitious but easier to test. A strong decision recognizes both strengths and limitations.\n\n**Try this:** Write two possible directions. For each, name one reason it matters, one constraint, and one way to narrow it. Which direction would you investigate first, and what could make you reconsider?\n\nUse this reading to revisit the three decision lenses. The interactive lesson and Gauntlet will give you practice, and the Project Direction Brief will show how you use the lenses for your own idea.\n\nContinue to Your Starting Point. Return to this reading whenever you need support.\n\n### By the end of this module\n\n- Identify at least two interests or questions that could become a project.\n- Apply relevance, feasibility, and scope to compare two possible directions.\n- Justify a focused direction and explain a tradeoff in your Project Direction Brief.\n\nAfter the worked lesson, try the Gauntlet or use the included text case in Compare Two Directions. Carry a specific question from your Direction Brief into Module 2.\n\n### Plan your week\n\nDay 1 is when your facilitator opens this module. Share Compare Two Directions for formative feedback by Day 3. Submit the Project Direction Brief by Day 7 after considering any feedback. Check Course questions in Discussions for help with timing.","is_published":false},{"slug":"module-two-introduction","prior":"# Welcome to Module 2: Plan & Launch\n\nYou chose a direction. Now you'll turn it into a manageable first move. You'll learn planning concepts, study Maya's school-garden example, practice recognizing safe actions and useful evidence, and enter First Move Studio to apply the same ideas to a new situation. You'll create a First Move Card, receive feedback, and decide whether to revise or retain your plan. The Launch Portfolio will show what you tried—or the honest barrier you encountered—and the next decision you can justify.\n\n## Assigned reading — An Informative First Move\n\nA first action does not need to complete a project. Its job is to help you learn something useful while staying safe and realistic.\n\nBegin with your **purpose**: what are you trying to improve, create or understand? Next, identify **milestones**—at least three ordered stages that take you from preparation toward a possible outcome. A milestone tells you what progress could look like, not what must happen no matter what.\n\nThen list **resources** and **constraints**. Resources might include time, information, a workspace or permission from a responsible adult. Constraints are things that may limit your action: a busy schedule, unavailable materials, privacy concerns or school rules. A thoughtful plan includes what you would do if a constraint matters.\n\nChoose one **bounded action**. It should be small enough to do safely and informative enough to help you decide what comes next. For a school garden, the bounded action may be to ask a staff sponsor what permission is needed and sketch a possible single bed, not to start digging without permission.\n\nFinally, distinguish **prediction** from **evidence**. “I think students will like my idea” is a prediction. “Three people I asked could explain the draft clearly” is an observation, though the observation still cannot prove that everyone will like it. Be specific about what you actually learned and what remains uncertain.\n\nWhen feedback arrives, you do not have to agree automatically. Consider the comment, ask whether evidence supports it, and either revise your decision or explain why you are keeping it.\n\n**Try this:** For a project you care about, write one safe first action, one thing that could stop it, one possible observation and one thing that observation would **not** prove.\n\nRead this before Maya's worked example and the guided checks. Your actions must be safe, bounded and honest.\n\n### By the end of this module\n\n- Analyze how a purpose, ordered milestones, and constraints shape a plan.\n- Develop and revise a safe first move using facilitator feedback and actual evidence or an honest account of a barrier.\n- Explain what you learned, what remains uncertain, and one justified next move in your Launch Portfolio.\n\nFollow the module in order. Share the First Move Card with your facilitator. Return for the feedback before completing Respond to Feedback and acting on your plan.","next":"# Welcome to Module 2: Plan & Launch\n\nYou chose a direction. Now you'll turn it into a manageable first move. You'll learn planning concepts, study Maya's school-garden example, practice recognizing safe actions and useful evidence, and enter First Move Studio to apply the same ideas to a new situation. You'll create a First Move Card, receive feedback, and decide whether to revise or retain your plan. The Launch Portfolio will show what you tried—or the honest barrier you encountered—and the next decision you can justify.\n\n## Assigned reading — An Informative First Move\n\nA first action does not need to complete a project. Its job is to help you learn something useful while staying safe and realistic.\n\nBegin with your **purpose**: what are you trying to improve, create or understand? Next, identify **milestones**—at least three ordered stages that take you from preparation toward a possible outcome. A milestone tells you what progress could look like, not what must happen no matter what.\n\nThen list **resources** and **constraints**. Resources might include time, information, a workspace or permission from a responsible adult. Constraints are things that may limit your action: a busy schedule, unavailable materials, privacy concerns or school rules. A thoughtful plan includes what you would do if a constraint matters.\n\nChoose one **bounded action**. It should be small enough to do safely and informative enough to help you decide what comes next. For a school garden, the bounded action may be to ask a staff sponsor what permission is needed and sketch a possible single bed, not to start digging without permission.\n\nFinally, distinguish **prediction** from **evidence**. “I think students will like my idea” is a prediction. “Three people I asked could explain the draft clearly” is an observation, though the observation still cannot prove that everyone will like it. Be specific about what you actually learned and what remains uncertain.\n\nWhen feedback arrives, you do not have to agree automatically. Consider the comment, ask whether evidence supports it, and either revise your decision or explain why you are keeping it.\n\n**Try this:** For a project you care about, write one safe first action, one thing that could stop it, one possible observation and one thing that observation would **not** prove.\n\nRead this before Maya's worked example and the guided checks. Your actions must be safe, bounded and honest.\n\n### By the end of this module\n\n- Analyze how a purpose, ordered milestones, and constraints shape a plan.\n- Develop and revise a safe first move using facilitator feedback and actual evidence or an honest account of a barrier.\n- Explain what you learned, what remains uncertain, and one justified next move in your Launch Portfolio.\n\nFollow the module in order. Share the First Move Card with your facilitator. Return for the feedback before completing Respond to Feedback and acting on your plan.\n\n### Plan your week\n\nDay 1 is when your facilitator opens this module. Share your First Move Card by Day 3. Review the facilitator’s feedback and complete your revision or retention note by Day 5. Submit your Launch Portfolio by Day 7 after documenting a safe attempt or a genuine barrier. If feedback is late, save your draft and ask the facilitator how to proceed.","is_published":false}]$staged$::jsonb) as e(slug text,prior text,next text,is_published boolean),
       public.pll_courses c
  where c.slug='project-launch-lab' and a.course_id=c.id and a.slug=e.slug
    and a.instructions=e.prior and a.is_published=e.is_published
  returning a.id
 )
 select count(*) into updated_count from updated;
 if updated_count<>4 then raise exception 'Course copy or publication state changed before review.'; end if;
end
$night_polish$;